Technical Report
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

What Happened?
--------------

EXIF data was found on 3 out of the 5 photos on the ChirpyHub company account.
This data included GPS coordinates which were used in finding the location of
the CEO's barn. EXIF data is metadata that is automatically embedded in digital
photos once they are taken and can also contain data such as camera model and
camera settings.

Most smartphones automatically capture this data when taking pictures, but users
can turn off the setting that allows the smartphone to retain such data. Due to
this EXIF data, we are vulnerable on our site because our system was not
removing the data when uploaded. This error puts every user on the site at risk
of having their location data exposed, including our company as well.

----------------------------
How I Replicated This Issue.
----------------------------

I was able to replicate this issue by reading the JPG file using Python Imaging
Library (PIL). Using code, I was able to use PIL to get EXIF data using
`_getexif()`. Once I got the data, I had to convert it to decimal format so we
could read the data normally.

Once we were able to read it in decimal format, I was able to determine which
hemisphere it was in due to latitude and longitude having negative numbers.
Once we got the coordinates, we validated it to make sure we were getting a
number, then stored it in a CSV file.

That's when we discovered that 3 out of 5 images contained the EXIF data(Readable data)
that gave us the coordinates of where they were taken. Once we got the coordinates,
we used Mapmakerapp.com to pinpoint the photos' exact location. Getting this
data was not difficult, and any coding student should be able to recreate this
code to get the EXIF data like I did.

--------------------------------------------------
What Steps Could Be Taken to Remediate This Issue?
--------------------------------------------------

To start, ChirpyHub will begin stripping the metadata from users' photos when
they upload them and allow the user to tag their location if they want. This
would be the most beneficial approach as there will be no leak of private data,
and ChirpyHub will not be liable since we won't store the data unless the user
inputs the data themselves.

As for the data already on the site, we need to create a program to locate and
strip all images of their metadata to prevent any more leaks.